## Why This Is Asked
This question evaluates the ability to design a data catalog system with OTCA residency, lineage tracking, privacy masking, and cross-region compliance. It tests knowledge of OpenLineage integration, data modeling, privacy controls, and multi-region architecture—skills distinct from typical telemetry pipeline work.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-region OTCA isolation with per-tenant residency
- OpenLineage-based lineage propagation
- On-read privacy masking for sensitive metadata
- Regional versus central store architecture with audit trails

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode for regional catalog with privacy masking
class RegionalCatalog {
  constructor(region, tenantId) {
    this.region = region;
    this.tenantId = tenantId;
    this.privacyMasker = new PrivacyMasker();
  }

  async getMetadata(datasetId) {
    const metadata = await this.fetchMetadata(datasetId);
    return this.privacyMasker.mask(metadata, this.tenantId);
  }
}

// OpenLineage event emission for lineage tracking
function emitLineageEvent(jobName, inputs, outputs) {
  const event = new RunEvent({
    eventType: 'COMPLETE',
    job: new Job({ namespace: 'regional-catalog', name: jobName }),
    inputs: inputs.map(i => new Dataset({ namespace: 'otca', name: i })),
    outputs: outputs.map(o => new Dataset({ namespace: 'otca', name: o }))
  });
  openLineageClient.emit(event);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question explores a specialized angle: robotics telemetry in a multi-tenant OTCA stack, requiring candidates to balance privacy compliance, cost optimization, and real-time processing requirements.

## Key Concepts

- **Per-tenant residency**: Logical and physical isolation ensuring data never crosses tenant boundaries
- **Edge to regional streaming**: Kafka-based pipeline with regional aggregation points
- **PII redaction at ingestion**: Prevention-first approach rather than post-processing
- **Lightweight sampling and quotas**: Cost control mechanisms without complex infrastructure
- **Parquet partitioning**: Tenant/date organization enables efficient queries and lifecycle management

## Test Plan

**Privacy Tests**:
- Verify PII fields (location, robot_id patterns) are redacted/hashed before Kafka
- Confirm tenant isolation: cross-tenant data access should return zero results
- Validate retention expiry: data older than 7 days is not queryable

**QoS Tests**:
- End-to-end latency from robot to S3: target <= 60 seconds
- Quota enforcement: verify throttling when tenant exceeds configured limits
- Sampling accuracy: validate sample rate matches configured percentage

**Fleet Health Dashboard Tests**:
- Data freshness: verify all metrics update within 60-second window
- Robot status accuracy: boot events correlate with robot_id state changes
- Battery alerting: sensor_alert events with low battery_mv trigger notifications
- Task completion tracking: task_complete events calculate fleet utilization metrics

## Code Example

```javascript
// Event schema definition
const telemetryEvent = {
  tenant_id: string,
  event_type: 'robot_boot' | 'sensor_alert' | 'task_assignment' | 'task_complete',
  timestamp: string, // ISO 8601
  robot_id: string,
  location: { lat: number, lon: number },
  battery_mv: number, // millivolts
  latency_ms: number,
  firmware_version: string,
  payload: Map<string, any> // extensibility
};

// PII redaction at edge gateway
const redactPII = (event) => {
  const redacted = { ...event };
  redacted.robot_id = hashField(event.robot_id, event.tenant_id);
  redacted.location = fuzzLocation(event.location, event.tenant_id);
  return redacted;
};

// Tenant quota enforcement
const checkQuota = (tenant_id, event) => {
  const limit = tenantLimits.get(tenant_id);
  return tenantCounters.increment(tenant_id) <= limit;
};
```

## Why This Is Asked
Explores practical RT-level data governance in OTCA: retention, holds, residency, and auditability across regions.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant policy store; region-scoped purges
- Data tagging: retention_version, tombstones
- Holds vs purge: immutable logs, legal holds
- Storage: object-lock Parquet; Kafka retention
- Testing: synthetic holds, burst scenarios, cross-region audits

## Code Example
```javascript
// Simple eligibility check for purge
function eligibleForPurge(record, policy, currentDate) {
  const byTenant = policy.tenant_id === record.tenant_id;
  const byRegion = policy.region === record.region;
  const ageDays = (currentDate - new Date(record.createdAt)) / (1000*60*60*24);
  const notOnHold = !record.hold_id || !policy.hold_ids.includes(record.hold_id);
  return byTenant && byRegion && ageDays > policy.retention_days && notOnHold;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you audit deletion correctness across millions of records?
- How would you handle privacy-rights requests that require immediate data erasure despite holds?
